{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LVLrFkxa2NvyW879VRKuin","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd029-comm\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/comm: Compare Two Sorted Files Line by Line\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  macOS BSD comm lacks the --nocheck-order and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU\n6\t  comm. Scripts that compare sorted file pairs for set-intersection or set-difference\n7\t  operations depend on GNU comm behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n8\t  GNU reference binary (gcomm, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag\n9\t  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Read two sorted files and produce three-column output showing lines unique to file 1, unique to file 2, and common to both, matching GNU comm behavior.\n13\t  - G2: Support -1, -2, and -3 flags to suppress individual output columns.\n14\t  - G3: Support --check-order and --nocheck-order to control whether input ordering is validated.\n15\t  - G4: Support --output-delimiter to configure the column separator.\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Three-column comparison output\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: Must read file1 and file2 simultaneously, line by line. Lines present only in file1 are written in column 1 (no indentation). Lines present only in file2 are written in column 2 (one tab indent). Lines present in both are written in column 3 (two tabs indent).\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: Must process files in sorted order; when a line from file1 is lexicographically less than the current file2 line, it is a column-1 line. When file2's line is less, it is a column-2 line. Equal lines go to column 3.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: When one file is exhausted, all remaining lines from the other file go to the appropriate column (column 1 if file1 has lines left, column 2 if file2 has lines left).\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Comparison is byte-for-byte with LC_ALL=C (no locale collation). An optional newline at the end of the last line does not affect comparison.\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Column suppression flags (-1, -2, -3)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: -1 must suppress column 1 (lines unique to file1). The remaining columns shift left in indentation.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: -2 must suppress column 2 (lines unique to file2). The remaining columns shift left accordingly.\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: -3 must suppress column 3 (lines common to both files). Combining -1 -2 -3 produces no output.\n39\t      - R2.4:\n40\t          text: When a column is suppressed, the tab indentation for remaining columns adjusts so that the leftmost output column has no leading tab.\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Order checking and output delimiter\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: By default, comm checks that input files are sorted. When a line is encountered that is less than the previous line in the same file, comm prints a warning to stderr and continues.\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: --check-order makes the ordering check fatal; comm exits non-zero if input is not sorted.\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: --nocheck-order disables the sorting check entirely; no warning is printed even if input is out of order.\n51\t      - R3.4:\n52\t          text: --output-delimiter=STRING replaces the default tab character as the column separator. All column indentation levels use STRING instead of a tab.\n53\t\n54\t  R4:\n55\t    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n56\t    items:\n57\t      - R4.1:\n58\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully and no order violations occur.\n59\t      - R4.2:\n60\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr.\n61\t      - R4.3:\n62\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n63\t      - R4.4:\n64\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n65\t\n66\tnon_goals:\n67\t  - cmd/comm does not sort its inputs; both files must already be sorted before invocation.\n68\t  - cmd/comm does not implement locale-aware collation; comparison is byte-for-byte under LC_ALL=C.\n69\t\n70\tacceptance_criteria:\n71\t  - id: AC1\n72\t    criterion: \"comm on file1='a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' and file2='b\\\\nc\\\\nd\\\\n' produces the three-column output with 'a' in col1, 'd' in col2, and 'b','c' in col3, matching gcomm.\"\n73\t    traces:\n74\t      - R1.1\n75\t      - R1.2\n76\t      - R1.3\n77\t      - R1.4\n78\t  - id: AC2\n79\t    criterion: \"comm -12 on the same files produces only 'b\\\\nc\\\\n' (common lines), matching gcomm -12.\"\n80\t    traces:\n81\t      - R1.1\n82\t      - R1.2\n83\t      - R1.3\n84\t      - R1.4\n85\t  - id: AC3\n86\t    criterion: \"comm -3 on the same files produces 'a\\\\n\\\\td\\\\n' (unique lines), matching gcomm -3.\"\n87\t    traces:\n88\t      - R1.1\n89\t      - R1.2\n90\t      - R1.3\n91\t      - R1.4\n92\t  - id: AC4\n93\t    criterion: \"comm --output-delimiter=| uses | instead of tab for column separation, matching gcomm.\"\n94\t    traces:\n95\t      - R3.4\n96\t  - id: AC5\n97\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gcomm passes for all required test cases.\"\n98\t    traces:\n99\t      - R1.1\n100\t      - R1.2\n101\t      - R1.3\n102\t      - R1.4\n103\t  - id: AC6\n104\t    criterion: \"cmd/comm compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n105\t    traces:\n106\t      - R1.1\n107\t      - R1.2\n108\t      - R1.3\n109\t      - R1.4\n110\t      - R2.1\n111\t      - R2.2\n112\t      - R2.3\n113\t      - R2.4\n114\t      - R3.1\n115\t      - R3.2\n116\t      - R3.3\n117\t      - R3.4\n118\t      - R4.1\n119\t      - R4.2\n120\t      - R4.3\n121\t      - R4.4\n122\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"697e028e-4e9c-491c-b6d7-b43b0b550ea8","uuid":"7e964853-d5